ADS-020 — Conformance

Document ID: ADS-020
Title: Conformance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Conformance Model (CCFM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Conformance Model establishes the principles, requirements, assessment methods, and evidence necessary to determine whether an implementation correctly realizes the Atlas specifications while preserving canonical semantics, governance, interoperability, and engineering integrity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Conformance architecture
Conformance levels
Conformance assessment
Certification
Profiles
Evidence
Reporting
Governance

This specification applies to all Atlas implementations, products, services, and platforms.

3. Canonical Definition

Conformance is the demonstrable implementation of Atlas specifications in accordance with their normative requirements, verified through objective assessment and supported by verifiable evidence.

Conformance evaluates implementations.

It does not evaluate individual engineering objects.

4. Objectives

The Conformance Model SHALL:

Enable implementation certification.
Preserve semantic consistency.
Promote interoperability.
Support objective assessment.
Enable ecosystem trust.
Support profile-based certification.
Preserve implementation independence.
Support future evolution.
5. Conformance Principles

Every Atlas conformance assessment SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Objective

Assessments SHALL be evidence-based.

Repeatable

Equivalent implementations SHALL produce equivalent assessment results.

Transparent

Assessment criteria SHALL be publicly documented.

Traceable

Every assessment SHALL preserve supporting evidence.

Technology Independent

Conformance SHALL evaluate specification implementation rather than technology choices.

Profile-Based

Conformance MAY be evaluated against one or more implementation profiles.

6. Conformance Levels

Atlas defines five canonical conformance levels.

Level	Description
Level 1	Semantic Conformance (ACC)
Level 2	Architectural Conformance (AHS)
Level 3	Data Conformance (ADS)
Level 4	Policy & Engine Conformance (APS/AES)
Level 5	Full Atlas Platform Conformance

Higher levels inherit all lower-level requirements.

7. Conformance Scope

Conformance MAY be assessed for:

Individual components
Services
Engines
Platforms
Organizations
Runtime profiles
Integration profiles
Multi-agent ecosystems

The scope SHALL be explicitly declared.

8. Conformance Assessment

Every assessment SHALL define:

Property	Description
Assessment ID	Unique identifier
Scope	Subject of assessment
Applicable Specifications	Standards evaluated
Conformance Level	Required level
Assessment Method	Manual, automated, hybrid
Assessor	Responsible authority
Assessment Date	Evaluation timestamp
Outcome	Final result

Assessments SHALL be versioned.

9. Conformance Evidence

Conformance SHALL be supported by evidence including:

Validation reports
Compliance assessments
Test results
Audit records
Interoperability demonstrations
Security assessments
Certification artifacts
Traceability records

Evidence SHALL remain immutable.

10. Conformance Outcomes

Atlas defines the following canonical outcomes.

Outcome	Description
Fully Conformant	All mandatory requirements satisfied
Conditionally Conformant	Minor deviations accepted
Partially Conformant	Only selected specification subsets implemented
Non-Conformant	Mandatory requirements not satisfied
Pending Assessment	Evaluation incomplete

Outcomes SHALL identify applicable profiles and specification versions.

11. Certification

Successful conformance assessments MAY result in certification.

Certification SHALL define:

Certified implementation
Certified specification versions
Applicable profiles
Certification authority
Effective period
Renewal requirements
Revocation conditions

Certification SHALL reference immutable assessment evidence.

12. Conformance Profiles

Atlas supports profile-based conformance.

Examples:

Profile	Purpose
Core Atlas	ACC + AHS
Data Platform	ADS
Engineering Platform	ACC + AHS + ADS + AES
Digital Twin	Engineering interoperability
Multi-Agent	Agent coordination
Government	Regulatory deployment

Profiles SHALL be versioned.

13. Conformance Governance

Every assessment SHALL define:

Assessment authority
Certification authority
Appeal process
Renewal policy
Revocation policy
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain independent of implementation technology.

14. Conformance Quality

Every assessment SHALL satisfy:

Accuracy
Objectivity
Repeatability
Traceability
Transparency
Auditability
Consistency
Explainability

Quality SHALL be continuously improved.

15. Conformance Lifecycle
Requested
      │
Prepared
      │
Assessed
      │
Reviewed
      │
Certified
      │
Monitored
      │
Renewed
      │
Expired

Certification status SHALL remain publicly verifiable where appropriate.

16. Conformance Events

Conformance activities MAY generate Canonical Events.

Examples:

Assessment Started
Assessment Completed
Certification Issued
Certification Renewed
Certification Revoked
Conformance Updated

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Automated certification.
Continuous conformance monitoring.
AI-assisted implementation assessment.
Cross-standard certification.
Federated certification authorities.
Global Atlas certification registries.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical conformance semantics.

